                <pb facs="0160" n="70" sameAs="frus:frus1875v01/pg70"/>

                <head>No. 38.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Heap
                        to Mr. Hunter.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Consulate of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Tunis</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1875-06-14"><hi rendition="#italic">June</hi> 14, 1875</date>.
                        (Received July 8.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 210.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: A scientific commission, in charge of the
                    Marquis Antinoré, a distinguished African traveler, and Captain Baratieri, of
                    the engineer corps of the Italian army, has been sent by the Royal Geographical
                    Society of Italy to make a careful and thorough survey of the basin in the Great
                    Desert south of the Aures range, and to examine the barrier which separates it
                    from the sea at Gabes. Another party was sent some time since by the French
                    government to make a similar survey south of Biskra, in the province of Algeria.
                    It is how likely that this interesting question will soon be definitively
                    solved. It is still thought, however, that the obstacles to the creation of an
                    inland sea at this point are insuperable.</p>
                <p>I am, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>G. H. HEAP.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
